WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.720
The first film showed how extracellular vesicles face outward and seal cytosol

2
00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:13.660
inside. Yet when a centrifuge pellet changes a target cell, how can you tell a

3
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:14.660
vesicle did the work?

4
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.680
Spinning cell fluid collects far more than vesicles.

5
00:00:19.783 --> 00:00:25.203
To collect small vesicles, researchers spin fluid at rising speeds, finishing at

6
00:00:25.203 --> 00:00:30.543
high speed. That spin sorts by size and density, not by identity.

7
00:00:31.863 --> 00:00:38.403
So the pellet also holds protein clumps, lipoproteins, RNA protein complexes and

8
00:00:38.403 --> 00:00:40.043
calf serum vesicles from the medium.

9
00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:44.587
Step one is separating vesicles from denser debris.

10
00:00:46.347 --> 00:00:51.047
Researchers load the pellet at the bottom of a dense sucrose gradient and spin until

11
00:00:51.047 --> 00:00:52.247
particles stop moving.

12
00:00:53.687 --> 00:00:55.187
Why do vesicles float upward?

13
00:00:56.627 --> 00:01:01.967
Membrane lipid is less dense than protein, so lipid-rich vesicles rise while free

14
00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:03.087
proteins stay below.

15
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:09.860
Next, test every layer on target cells, alongside a mock pellet spun from cell-free

16
00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:16.480
medium. If vesicles carry the signal, activity tracks the vesicle layer rather than

17
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:22.820
denser protein. Even then, some lipoproteins share that buoyant zone, so separation

18
00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:24.260
is only the first check.

19
00:01:25.283 --> 00:01:29.643
Step two asks whether cargo sits outside or inside the membrane.

20
00:01:31.043 --> 00:01:34.043
Treat the vesicles with a protease or a ribonuclease.

21
00:01:35.583 --> 00:01:40.503
Surface proteins get cut away, while sealed cargo survives behind the intact

22
00:01:40.503 --> 00:01:46.563
membrane. Adding detergent breaks the membrane, so internal cargo disappears too.

23
00:01:47.823 --> 00:01:51.023
One caution: bound proteins can shield loose RNA.

24
00:01:52.283 --> 00:01:56.823
Step three is counting, because tiny vesicles hold very little volume.

25
00:01:58.023 --> 00:02:08.523
A sphere 100 nm across encloses at most about 5 × 10⁻¹⁹ L. Multiply that volume by 1

26
00:02:08.523 --> 00:02:12.383
µM, then by Avogadro's number of molecules per mole.

27
00:02:13.883 --> 00:02:17.723
Passive trapping gives roughly 0.3 molecules per vesicle.

28
00:02:18.767 --> 00:02:23.687
Copies per vesicle equals cargo copies divided by particles counted.

29
00:02:25.207 --> 00:02:30.687
Suppose a purified sample holds two billion vesicles and forty million copies of one

30
00:02:30.687 --> 00:02:37.687
micro RNA. Does the average vesicle hold many copies, about one copy, or far less

31
00:02:37.687 --> 00:02:38.687
than one?

32
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:49.610
Far less than one, because forty million divided by two billion is zero point zero

33
00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:53.980
two. That is just one copy in every fifty vesicles.

34
00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:01.060
Counts in real samples also put a given micro RNA far below one copy per vesicle.

35
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:06.140
A vesicle surface, by contrast, displays many ligand copies.

36
00:03:07.233 --> 00:03:12.453
Low averages do not rule out internal signalling, since a rare subset might carry

37
00:03:12.453 --> 00:03:18.333
most copies. They mean any claim needs realistic doses and honest controls.

38
00:03:19.793 --> 00:03:24.813
Proving vesicle signalling requires separating vesicles, locating cargo, and

39
00:03:24.813 --> 00:03:25.633
counting copies.
